    "summary": "High‑energy Dark Star > Morning Dew and tight Half‑Step jams",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a soaring Dark Star → Morning Dew climax, a rollicking Mississippi Half-Step > Uptown Toodeloo, and a beautiful Bird Song. Jerry’s guitar is on fire while Phil provides crisp bass flourishes and the rhythm section stays locked in tight. The Miller soundboard recording is excellent, capturing every nuance of this 1972 performance.",
    "review": "The August 24, 1972 Berkeley Community Theater concert finds the Dead riding a high‑energy wave that never lets up. From the opening drive of \"The Promised Land\" through a punchy first set, the band builds momentum that explodes in the second set with a mesmerizing Dark Star → Morning Dew that showcases Jerry’s soaring leads and Phil’s bass‑driven propulsion. The Mississippi Half-Step > Uptown Toodeloo is a standout, with tight interplay between guitar, bass, and Keith’s keyboard textures, while Bird Song soars as one of the night’s most melodic peaks. The encore’s \"Uncle John’s Band\" wraps the evening in sweet familiarity. Thanks to the Miller soundboard source—remastered for crystal‑clear balance—the performance shines with exceptional clarity, making this a must‑listen snapshot of the band’s summer ’72 groove.",
